vectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348385 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348385 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348385 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348385 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f9c3543-a306-5635-8fa9-149cba24aebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348385 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348390 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a \\\\ -2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb9e7fcc-e638-502e-8bf0-7fbc40ad862a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348390 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348395 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348395 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348395 #edb/ref 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cac09b37-360a-5efb-9630-b76644cac8f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348395 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3ab5a48-4ec0-5b84-8cff-a429fb97fc59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186379816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95cac241-a6a2-5133-bd5b-0bc96d341951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379812 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379812 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379812 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bf3be306-cf4b-505f-9749-8c4d421cf8bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379813 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379813 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379813 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "675738a1-186a-5034-b012-faa1a6463030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379814 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379814 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379814 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2d147f9-5e98-5cd7-a62a-3b200ed85486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379815 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379815 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379815 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40744a34-ee79-5f47-96a8-dad2d3f736ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379816 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ is not diagonalizable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379816 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379816 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7371e87-7e47-503f-b703-0bb0dbb9110d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404107 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7be4462e-3d0b-5745-b83e-db8e5d74f57e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404107 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404108 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "958185d9-bfb3-5c99-9580-566f3546885e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404109 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404109 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404109 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15560ba1-c0e7-57a3-bd24-dc19e7e9f52a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404110 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404110 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404110 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a752dce-688f-5fd3-becb-69a53449004c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404111 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligne