=-2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404115 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5324a7ce-971d-53cb-b107-19a127a08e1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404116 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404116 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404116 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20692b0c-ecf3-5857-b371-5e00dd088456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404117 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ is not diagonalizable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8297858c-6a75-5e90-8004-887fd5f4fe6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404118 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404118 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404118 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21bdb776-d042-5bc3-8def-4d142fb67170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404119 #edb/ref 10